WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:07.480
There are a series of bills popping up in
a bunch of states that would criminalize and

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.099
repress protests of all types.

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:13.130
And this is really chilling.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.119
Red alert, authoritarian type stuff.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:20.650
In Nashville, Tennessee, lawmakers have put
together a bill eight thousand five eight

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.559
zero zero five, that Senate bill and House
Bill eight zero zero five already passed.

00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:32.140
The legislature would make it a class E felony
to camp on state public or a state or public

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.600
property between 10:00 p.m. and 7:00 a.m..

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.190
Now, what's the idea there?

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:44.740
The idea is have carte blanche, have a blank
slate to go after protests that will occupy

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.740
a plaza for some time or a park for some time.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.600
Once 10:00 p.m. comes classi felony.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.140
Start arresting everybody.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.920
They are threatening convictions punishable
by up to six years in prison and the possible

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.489
loss of your voting rights.

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:07.440
It makes it easier to charge protesters with
theft if they mark up state buildings with

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.440
chalk.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.610
A Michigan bill will classify rioting as terrorism.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.640
Now, what's the problem there?

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.030
The line on rioting is in and of itself up
for debate.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:30.130
This would make anything that you can call
rioting, even if it's not terrorism.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:38.049
It would make acts of, quote, social and domestic
anarchy punishable by up to 20 years in prison

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:41.960
or a 250000 dollar fine in South Dakota.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:48.619
The governor signed into law stricter penalties
for rioting and inciting a riot.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:56.390
Now, inciting a riot is pernicious since animated
protesting could conceivably look like it

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.900
incited someone to start rioting.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:05.140
And that's a really broad catchall that is
being applied there in Louisiana.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.909
There's a bill on the table which could mean
15 years in prison for protesting, quote,

00:02:09.909 --> 00:02:11.639
on critical infrastructure.

00:02:11.639 --> 00:02:18.900
What's the idea there to repress protests
near a pipeline that residents in Louisiana

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.900
oppose?

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.519
Now, there are many more examples.

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:26.159
More than 20 states over the last couple of
years have proposed some of these types of

00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:27.159
bills.

00:02:27.159 --> 00:02:31.230
The idea is sometimes to criminalize homelessness.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.549
Sometimes it's to criminalize protesting.

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:38.859
Other times it's to restrict, restrict speech
in practice, although they claim that's not

00:02:38.859 --> 00:02:40.560
what they're doing in theory.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.129
And this is the stuff that reminds us why
we need to vote at every level.

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:49.419
It's not just presidential, but we need state
senates, state legislatures, governorships,

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:52.909
because those are the origins of a lot of
these bills.

00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:58.819
And those are people choosing many of the
state judges that might eventually decide

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:04.739
on the legality of some of these authoritarian
restrictions on protesting.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:08.340
Authoritarianism really does not like protesting.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.400
We know that from history now.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.540
I think it's also important to remember that
this is not just about new laws.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.129
You can weaponize existing laws as well, which
is why we need the right mayors and police

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:21.719
chiefs in place.

00:03:21.719 --> 00:03:26.799
Mayors often choosing police chiefs because
they can take advantage of existing curfew

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:27.799
laws.

00:03:27.799 --> 00:03:32.709
They can focus on unlawful assembly angles,
on gatherings.

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:37.980
And on and on, so it's not just about state
legislatures, state senators and governors,

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.319
we need the right mayors.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:41.499
We need to push them to select the right police
chiefs.

00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:44.519
This is much more than the presidency.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:50.829
By the way, it's also really weird and of
course, completely hypocritical that many

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:57.029
of the same people stockpiling weapons in
case the government were to become tyrannical

00:03:57.029 --> 00:04:01.049
are in support of laws cracking down on unarmed
protesters.

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:05.729
On our saying, well, if the gatherings are
against the law, these protesters should find

00:04:05.729 --> 00:04:10.230
some other way to request redress of their
grievances.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.469
What's what?

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:15.010
Things are completely backwards, of course.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:26.970
And this is just the latest.

